{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"kind\":\"final\",\"decision\":\"answer\",\"answer\":754,\"evidence\":[\"receipt-1\"],\"confidence\":0.9,\"status\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"reason\":\"DP counting binary strings of length 13 avoiding 000 and 111\"}","reasoning_content":"We need count binary strings length 13 that avoid forbidden substrings \"000\" and \"111\". So no three consecutive same bits. Count sequences of length n with no run of 3 identical bits. Classic recurrence. Let a_n be number of length n ending with run of length 1 of some bit, b_n ending with run length 2. Actually we can use DP: states: last run length 1 of 0 or 1, and length 2 of 0 or 1. But symmetry: counts for 0 and 1 same. So let A_n = number of strings length n ending with a single bit (run length 1). B_n = number ending with double bit (run length 2). Then recurrence: To get A_{n+1}, we must append a bit different from last bit, so from any string of length n (regardless of run length) we can append opposite bit, resulting run length 1. So A_{n+1} = total_n = A_n + B_n. To get B_{n+1}, we must append same bit as last bit, but only if last run length was 1 (since cannot extend run length 2). So B_{n+1} = A_n. Base: length1: strings \"0\",\"1\": both run length1. So A_1=2, B_1=0. Compute up to n=13. Let's iterate.\n\nn=1: A=2,B=0,total=2\nn=2: A2 = total1 =2; B2 = A1=2; total2=4\nn=3: A3 = total2=4; B3 = A2=2; total3=6\nn=4: A4 = total3=6; B4 = A3=4; total4=10\nn=5: A5 = total4=10; B5 = A4=6; total5=16\nn=6: A6 = total5=16; B6 = A5=10; total6=26\nn=7: A7 = total6=26; B7 = A6=16; total7=42\nn=8: A8 = total7=42; B8 = A7=26; total8=68\nn=9: A9 = total8=68; B9 = A8=42; total9=110\nn=10: A10 = total9=110; B10 = A9=68; total10=178\nn=11: A11 = total10=178; B11 = A10=110; total11=288\nn=12: A12 = total11=288; B12 = A11=178; total12=466\nn=13: A13 = total12=466; B13 = A12=288; total13=754\n\nThus answer 754."}}],"created":1790952068,"model":"C:\\Users\\d2j